#
How to Change an Existing User's Role

To change a team member's permissions, you do not need to rewrite your whole setup. You only need to modify the role_definition_name text string inside your code, review the change, and apply it.

Follow these 4 steps to update a role safely:
#
Step 1: Open Your Infrastructure Code

1. Open your project folder in VS Code.

2. Open the file located at: infrastructure/main.tf.

#
Step 2: Edit the Role Name in the Code
#
Find the permission block you want to change, and edit the text inside the quotation marks next to role_definition_name.

#
Example - Downgrading to Read-Only: If your specialist no longer needs deletion powers, change "Contributor" to "Reader":
#
resource "azurerm_role_assignment" "delete_wasteful_resources" {
  scope                = data.azurerm_resource_group.target_group.id
  role_definition_name = "Reader" # ◄── Type the new role name here
  principal_id         = data.azuread_user.finops_specialist.object_id
}
#
Step 3: Preview the Change in the Terminal
Before pushing changes live to Azure, always preview them to make sure you didn't make a typo.

Open your VS Code terminal and move into your infrastructure folder:
#
cd infrastructure
#
Run the preview command:
#
terraform plan
#
What to look for: Terraform will look at Azure and print a preview showing that it will destroy 1 old role assignment and create 1 new role assignment with a green + and yellow ~ symbol.

#
Step 4: Apply the New Role Live
If the preview looks correct, commit the new role assignment directly to your live Azure cloud account:
#
bash
Run the deploy command:
#
terraform apply
#
When the terminal asks you to confirm, type yes and press Enter.
Azure will instantly strip the old permissions away from the specialist's account and activate their brand-new role!

#
Common Error: Role Definition Not Found
If the terminal turns red and prints a RoleDefinitionNotFound error during Step 3 or 4, it means the role name was misspelled. Azure role names are case-sensitive and must match official naming conventions exactly.
#
Always double-check your spelling against this quick list of core FinOps roles:

1. "Reader" (Safe, view-only access)
2. "Contributor" (Full creation, editing, and deletion access)
3. "Cost Management Reader" (Financial billing views only)
4. "Virtual Machine Contributor" (Stop, start, and resize VMs)

